2186121193 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445348 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186123008 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123009 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124373 1071 "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" 13194139534950 17592186045417 false] [17592186124373 1071 "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\nIV. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445387 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445388 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445389 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445383 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445385 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445384 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445386 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124718 1050 780.0 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124762 1071 "Given that $y < 150$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" 13194139534950 17592186045417 false] [17592186124762 1071 "Given that $y<150$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124762 1074 "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5(150-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.5,$ and $a=150.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<150$ for all $t\\ge 0.$" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121175 13194139534950 17592186045417 false] [17592186124766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124766 1071 "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $units/day$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534950 17592186045417 false] [17592186124766 1071 "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $\\text{units/day}$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186124766 1074 "The rate at which the production $P(t)$ of the factory changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=70\\text{ units/day},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P).\n$$" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186125068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186125068 1071 "A poultry farmer has egg-laying chickens. She knows that her farm can produce a maximum of $500$ eggs a day; however, it is far behind it.\nSince she started monitoring, the chickens laid $390$ eggs and $400$ eggs on days $3$ and $6$, respectively. How many eggs do the chickens lay on day $42$?\nRound your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the egg production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139534950 17592186045417 false] [17592186125068 1071 "A poultry farmer has egg-laying chickens. She knows that her farm can produce a maximum of $500$ eggs a day; however, it is far behind it.\nSince she started monitoring, the chickens laid $390$ eggs and $400$ eggs on days $3$ and $6,$ respectively. How many eggs do the chickens lay on day $42?$\nRound your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the egg production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186125068 1074 "The rate at which the egg production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible egg production and its current egg production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the farm, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500$ eggs, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(500-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{500-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{500-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|500-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $500-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}500-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 500-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 500-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the egg production was $390$ eggs. So, applying the condition $E(3)=390,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(3) & =390 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-3k} & =390 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =110.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $6,$ the egg production was $400$ eggs. So, applying the condition $E(6)=400,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(6) & =400 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-6k} & =400 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-6k}} & =\\frac{110}{100} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{11}{10} \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{11}{10}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{11}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)} & =110 \\\\ K & =110{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)} \\\\ K & =121\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=500-121{e}^{-(t/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of eggs laid on day $t=42$ is\n\n$$\nE(42)=500-121{e}^{-(42/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)}≈468\\,\\text{eggs},\n$$\n\n rounded to nearest integer." 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186125711 1064 "Our general solution for the wolf population in month $t$ is $P(t) = 450 - Ke^{-kt}.$ Now, let's find the specific values of our constants.\n\nWe're told that, in month $6,$ the population was $392.$ So, applying the condition $P(6) = 392,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & =392 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-6k} & =392 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, in month $12,$ the population was $420.$ So, applying the condition $P(12) = 420,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =420 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-12k} & =420 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{58}{30} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{29}{15} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{29}{15}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{29}{15}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =58{e}^{6k} \\\\  & =58(\\frac{29}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1682}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wolf population in month $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 450 - \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-(t/6)\\ln(29/15)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let [math]m[/math] be the month when the population reaches [math]435[/math] wolves. Then, we have\n\n\\begin{align*}\n435 &= 450 - \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-km} \\\\[5pt]\n15 &= \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-km} \\\\[5pt]\ne^{-km} &= \\frac{225}{1682} \\\\[5pt]\n-km &= \\ln \\left(\\frac{225}{1682}\\right) \\\\[5pt]\nm &= -\\frac{1}{k}\\ln \\left(\\frac{225}{1682}\\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nSubstituting in our value for [math]k,[/math] we conclude that the population will be [math]435[/math] wolves after approximately\n\\[\nm = \\dfrac{-\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{225}{1682}\\right)}{\\dfrac{1}{6}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{29}{15}\\right)}\n=18.3\\ldots\n\\approx 18\n\\]\nmonths, rounded to the nearest month." 13194139534950 17592186045417 false] [17592186125711 1064 "Our general solution for the wolf population in month $t$ is $P(t) = 450 - Ke^{-kt}.$ Now, let's find the specific values of our constants.\n\nWe're told that, in month $6,$ the population was $392.$ So, applying the condition $P(6) = 392,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & =392 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-6k} & =392 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, in month $12,$ the population was $420.$ So, applying the condition $P(12) = 420,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =420 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-12k} & =420 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{58}{30} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{29}{15} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{29}{15}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{29}{15}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =58{e}^{6k} \\\\  & =58(\\frac{29}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1682}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wolf population in month $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 450 - \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-(t/6)\\ln(29/15)}.\n$$" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186125810 1064 "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited growth models, let’s work through an example to see how they are used in practice.\n\nSuppose a wildlife reserve is tracking the population of a reintroduced wolf pack. The environment can support a maximum population of $450$ wolves, but the current population is still below this limit. Since monitoring began, the population reached $392$ wolves after $6$ months and $420$ wolves after $12$ months.\n\nLet's construct an inhibited growth model to describe the wolf population in terms of the month $t.$\n\nThe rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the reserve can sustain, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=450,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(450-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{450-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{450-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}|450-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $450 - P > 0$ for all $t \\geq 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}450-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 450-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 450-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =450-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nThis is the general solution. On the next slide, we will use the population data to find the specific values for the constants $K$ and $k.$" 13194139534950 17592186045417 false] [17592186125810 1064 "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited growth models, let’s work through an example to see how they are used in practice.\n\nSuppose a wildlife reserve is tracking the population of a reintroduced wolf pack. The environment can support a maximum population of $450$ wolves, but the current population is still below this limit. Since monitoring began, the population reached $392$ wolves after $6$ months and $420$ wolves after $12$ months.\n\nLet's construct an inhibited growth model to describe the wolf population in terms of the month $t.$\n\nThe rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the reserve can sustain, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=450,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(450-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{450-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{450-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|450-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $450 - P > 0$ for all $t \\geq 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}450-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 450-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 450-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =450-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nThis is the general solution. On the next slide, we will use the population data to find the specific values for the constants $K$ and $k.$" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186125811 1064 "In many real situations, a quantity increases quickly at first, but then the increase slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, suppose a cold drink is taken out of a refrigerator and placed in a warm room. At first, its temperature rises rapidly because the difference between the drink's temperature and the room temperature is large. As the drink warms up, this difference decreases, and the warming slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited growth model**.\n\nAn *inhibited growth equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ increases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P < a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as an upper bound. The solution increases over time but never rises above $a$, instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/81/815441d6991917ade1594f1c60a90e9505577917b1088cae3b10c637b4486c15.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited growth models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited growth, we factorise out the *positive* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 60-12P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited growth equation for $P < 5$ and $t \\geq 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=12$ and $a=5$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 12(5-P).\n$$" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186125811 1064 "In many real situations, a quantity increases quickly at first, but then the increase slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, suppose a cold drink is taken out of a refrigerator and placed in a warm room. At first, its temperature rises rapidly because the difference between the drink's temperature and the room temperature is large. As the drink warms up, this difference decreases, and the warming slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited growth model**.\n\nAn *inhibited growth equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ increases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P < a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as an upper bound. The solution increases over time but never rises above $a$, instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/81/815441d6991917ade1594f1c60a90e9505577917b1088cae3b10c637b4486c15.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited growth models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited growth, we factorise out the *positive* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 60-12P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited growth equation for $P < 5$ and $t \\geq 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=12$ and $a=5$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 12(5-P).\n$$\n\nNow let's work through a few examples to get some practice identifying inhibited growth models." 13194139534950 17592186045417 false] [17592186445331 1080 "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(120-V)" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186445331 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186445331 1083 #uuid "043dc448-2d64-5bd5-b613-9351f25fcbe7" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186445332 1080 "$19\\text{th}$ day" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186445332 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186445332 1083 #uuid "05356339-ef8c-55b3-b26e-1d5a396dcc30" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186445333 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(70-P)" 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] [17592186445333 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534950 17592186045417 true] 